{
  "title": "📊 Arithmetic Progressions — Patterns in Numbers",
  "hook": "You're saving money: ₹100 in week 1, ₹150 in week 2, ₹200 in week 3... Each week you save ₹50 more. This is an Arithmetic Progression — a sequence where you add the same number each time!",
  "explanation": "<div style=\"background:#F3E5F5;border-left:4px solid #7B1FA2;border-radius:8px;padding:12px 16px;margin:12px 0\"><strong style=\"color:#7B1FA2\">Arithmetic Progression (AP):</strong> A sequence a₁, a₂, a₃, ... where each term differs from the previous by a constant <b>d</b> (common difference).</div><div style=\"background:linear-gradient(135deg,#E3F2FD,#BBDEFB);border-left:4px solid #1565C0;border-radius:8px;padding:12px 16px;margin:12px 0;font-family:'Courier New',monospace;font-size:1.05rem;text-align:center\"><div style=\"font-size:0.7rem;text-transform:uppercase;font-weight:700;color:#1565C0;margin-bottom:4px\">Common Difference</div><strong>d = a₂ − a₁ = a₃ − a₂ = ... = aₙ − aₙ₋₁</strong></div><table style=\"width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;margin:12px 0;font-size:0.88rem\"><tr><th style=\"background:#E3F2FD;padding:8px 12px;border:1px solid #BBDEFB;text-align:left;font-weight:700\">Sequence</th><th style=\"background:#E3F2FD;padding:8px 12px;border:1px solid #BBDEFB;text-align:left;font-weight:700\">a (first term)</th><th style=\"background:#E3F2FD;padding:8px 12px;border:1px solid #BBDEFB;text-align:left;font-weight:700\">d</th><th style=\"background:#E3F2FD;padding:8px 12px;border:1px solid #BBDEFB;text-align:left;font-weight:700\">Is it AP?</th></tr><tr><td style=\"padding:8px 12px;border:1px solid #E0E0E0\">2, 5, 8, 11, ...</td><td style=\"padding:8px 12px;border:1px solid #E0E0E0\">2</td><td style=\"padding:8px 12px;border:1px solid #E0E0E0\">+3</td><td style=\"padding:8px 12px;border:1px solid #E0E0E0\">Yes ✓</td></tr><tr><td style=\"padding:8px 12px;border:1px solid #E0E0E0\">10, 7, 4, 1, −2, ...</td><td style=\"padding:8px 12px;border:1px solid #E0E0E0\">10</td><td style=\"padding:8px 12px;border:1px solid #E0E0E0\">−3</td><td style=\"padding:8px 12px;border:1px solid #E0E0E0\">Yes ✓ (d can be negative!)</td></tr><tr><td style=\"padding:8px 12px;border:1px solid #E0E0E0\">5, 5, 5, 5, ...</td><td style=\"padding:8px 12px;border:1px solid #E0E0E0\">5</td><td style=\"padding:8px 12px;border:1px solid #E0E0E0\">0</td><td style=\"padding:8px 12px;border:1px solid #E0E0E0\">Yes ✓ (d can be zero!)</td></tr><tr><td style=\"padding:8px 12px;border:1px solid #E0E0E0\">1, 4, 9, 16, ...</td><td style=\"padding:8px 12px;border:1px solid #E0E0E0\">1</td><td style=\"padding:8px 12px;border:1px solid #E0E0E0\">varies</td><td style=\"padding:8px 12px;border:1px solid #E0E0E0\">No ✗ (differences: 3,5,7 — not constant)</td></tr></table><div style=\"background:#F5F5F5;border-left:3px solid #4A90D9;border-radius:6px;padding:8px 12px;margin:6px 0;font-size:0.9rem\">An AP is fully described by just two things: the <b>first term a</b> and the <b>common difference d</b>.</div><div style=\"background:#FFF8E1;border-left:4px solid #FFB300;border-radius:8px;padding:10px 14px;margin:10px 0;font-size:0.88rem\">💡 <strong>Tip:</strong> To check if a sequence is an AP: compute all consecutive differences. If they're all the same → it's an AP!</div>",
  "worked_example": "<b>Example:</b> Is −3, −1, 1, 3, 5 an AP? If so, find the common difference.<br><br><div style=\"margin:12px 0\"><div style=\"display:flex;gap:12px;margin-bottom:10px;align-items:flex-start\"><div style=\"min-width:32px;height:32px;border-radius:50%;background:#4A90D9;color:white;display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:center;font-weight:700;font-size:0.85rem\">1</div><div><strong>Find differences</strong><br><span style=\"color:#555;font-size:0.88rem\">−1 − (−3) = 2, 1 − (−1) = 2, 3 − 1 = 2, 5 − 3 = 2</span></div></div><div style=\"display:flex;gap:12px;margin-bottom:10px;align-items:flex-start\"><div style=\"min-width:32px;height:32px;border-radius:50%;background:#4A90D9;color:white;display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:center;font-weight:700;font-size:0.85rem\">2</div><div><strong>All differences equal?</strong><br><span style=\"color:#555;font-size:0.88rem\">Yes, all are 2</span></div></div><div style=\"display:flex;gap:12px;margin-bottom:10px;align-items:flex-start\"><div style=\"min-width:32px;height:32px;border-radius:50%;background:#4A90D9;color:white;display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:center;font-weight:700;font-size:0.85rem\">3</div><div><strong>Conclusion</strong><br><span style=\"color:#555;font-size:0.88rem\">It's an AP with a = −3 and d = 2</span></div></div></div>",
  "try_this": {
    "question": "Check if 1, 3, 6, 10, 15 is an AP.",
    "hint": "Find differences: 3−1, 6−3, 10−6, 15−10.",
    "answer": "Differences: 2, 3, 4, 5. Not constant! This is NOT an AP. (It's actually the sequence of triangular numbers.)"
  },
  "fun_fact": "The concept of AP was known to ancient Babylonians around 1700 BC! They used it to predict astronomical events like eclipses."
}